      "name": null,
      "problem": "If $|11-4x|=3,$ then $x=$",
      "worked_solution": "Either the positive or negative of the expression inside the absolute value must be equal to the right-hand side of the equation.\n\nSo, we set up the two corresponding equations and solve them independently:\n\n- If $|11-4x|=11-4x,$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|11-4x| & =3 \\\\ 11-4x & =3 \\\\ -4x & =-8 \\\\ \\frac{-4x}{-4} & =\\frac{-8}{-4} \\\\ x & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- If $|11-4x|=-(11-4x),$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|11-4x| & =3 \\\\ -(11-4x) & =3 \\\\ -11+4x & =3 \\\\ 4x & =14 \\\\ \\frac{4x}{4} & =\\frac{14}{4} \\\\ x & =\\frac{7}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x=2$ and $x=\\frac{7}{2}.$",
